---
/* WHY WE EXIST — rebuilt 29 Oct. Client: "the layout and sequence is
  terrible .. there's so much happening and there's a lot of cognitive
  load."

  The copy was never the problem. It is a five-beat argument that turns:
  origin, room A fails, room B fails, the bind, the third room. The old
  build buried that sequence inside a scroll-scrubbed "aperture" that
  opened width-then-height and uncovered each line as you scrolled, with
  the film looping beside it the whole time. Diagnosed against this
  project's own design_process doctrine, that build broke five rules at
  once -- the full list is in home.css above the .why block. The two that
  matter most here are structural:

    "No essential information is conveyed ONLY through animation"
    (awwwards-motion, accessibility check). Every line of the argument was
    clip-path-hidden until scroll, so the section could not be read at
    rest. That IS the cognitive load: you cannot read ahead, and at any
    single scroll position you are looking at a half-opened box.

    "No competing motion (two elements fighting for attention at the same
    time)" (awwwards-motion, composition check). The looping film ran
    beside text that was itself animating in.

  So: one vertical column, five beats, each a single idea, all of it
  readable standing still. 04-layouts' own filter row ("editorial,
  portfolio, art-directed -> One Column") picks the archetype, and the
  catalogue describes One Column as "one thing at a time", which is
  exactly what was asked for.

  The layout carries the argument rather than decorating it -- what the
  aperture was reaching for and missed. The two rooms are the section's
  only two-column moment, deliberately EQUAL and split by a rule, because
  the copy's whole point is that neither room won. The third room breaks
  that symmetry into one dominant block with the film beside it. Symmetry
  resolving into asymmetry is the turn, and it is legible without
  scrolling.

  Assets all stay, placed where each is earned instead of running at once:
  the two stills sit with the origin (people in a room, which is what the
  line says), the film sits with the resolution (the room that got built).
  Nothing shares a beat with the film, so nothing competes with it.

  --- 2 Nov, second pass. Client: "i need to be able to understand the
  message being passed."

  The five beats were right and the vertical column was right, but the
  section rendered as two monuments with a whisper between them. Beats 1
  and 5 were set at h3 and h2; beats 2, 3 and 4 -- which carry the actual
  reason the Glow Tribe exists -- were short grey sentences stretched
  across a 1184px measure with a 104px void above and below each. The eye
  jumped monument to monument and the argument in the middle was never
  read. The visitor got "it started in Warri" and "we made a third room"
  and no idea what happened in between.

  THE FIX IS THE METAPHOR. The copy is about ROOMS -- two that didn't
  work and a third we built. So the layout is now three rooms you can
  count:

    01 and 02 are OUTLINED plates, side by side, sharing a wall. Hairline
       border, no fill: rooms that existed but stood empty for us.
    03 is a FILLED plate spanning the width both of them share. Fill
       against outline is the whole argument in one glance, and a ground
       change is what DESIGN.md Section 4 names as this system's way of
       expressing depth -- not a shadow, not a glow.

  Each room is numbered and named, so the sequence is explicit rather
  than inferred from reading order. The bind sits inside the problem
  block with the two rooms rather than floating a full section-gap away,
  because "here are the two rooms" and "here is what they cost you" are
  one thought and the gap was cutting it in half.

  Nothing about the copy changed. */
---
<section class="why g-ink" id="why" data-fade aria-labelledby="why-k">
  <div class="why__inner">
    <!-- the old markup had no heading element at all, so the section had
         no accessible name. Same eyebrow styling, real heading. -->
    <h2 class="eyebrow why__k" id="why-k">Why we exist</h2>

    <!-- BEAT 1 — the origin, told and shown. The two stills are joined
         into one object so they read as one room, not two photographs. -->
    <div class="why__origin">
      <p class="why__lead">It started with a handful of people in a room in Warri,
         trading half-finished songs and rough cuts.</p>
      <!-- 3 Nov: the client's own room shots. Landscape, so they stack
           rather than sit side by side -- a 4:5 crop cut half the room out
           of each one, and the room is the subject. -->
      <div class="why__shots">
        <img src="/img/why-01.webp" alt="" width="1040" height="695" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
        <img src="/img/why-02.webp" alt="" width="1040" height="695" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- BEATS 2-4 — THE PROBLEM. One unit, because the two rooms and the
         bind are one thought: here are the two rooms, and here is what
         they cost you. Splitting them across the section's big gap was
         what made the middle of the argument disappear. -->
    <div class="why__problem">
      <div class="why__rooms">
        <div class="why__room">
          <p class="why__roomk"><span>01</span> The craft room</p>
          <p class="why__roomline">The places that took the craft seriously didn&rsquo;t know what to do
             with the faith.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="why__room">
          <p class="why__roomk"><span>02</span> The faith room</p>
          <p class="why__roomline">The places that took the faith seriously didn&rsquo;t take the craft
             seriously at all.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- BEAT 4 — the bind, directly under both rooms and tied to them by
           proximity rather than by a connector graphic. -->
      <p class="why__bind">Everyone we knew was choosing between two rooms, and losing something
         in whichever one they picked.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- BEAT 5 — the third room. The two rooms above are OUTLINES: rooms
         that existed but stood empty for us. This one is FILLED, and it
         spans the width both of them share. The fill is the whole point,
         and it is the ground-change DESIGN.md Section 4 names as this
         system's way of expressing depth -- no shadow, no border trick. -->
    <section class="why__turn" aria-label="The third room">
      <div class="why__turncol">
        <p class="why__roomk why__roomk--third"><span>03</span> The room we built</p>
        <div>
          <!-- no forced <br>: type.css gives this text-wrap: balance, and the
               hard break fought it into four lines with an awkward "So we
               stopped / choosing," split. Balanced, it lands in three. -->
          <p class="why__resolve">So we stopped choosing, and made a third room.</p>
          <p class="why__tail">That&rsquo;s all The Glow Tribe is: the room we couldn&rsquo;t find.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <!-- preload="none" and poster-first: 3MB on a Nigerian mobile
           connection (SC7) is not something to spend before a visitor has
           reached the section. home-hero-and-sections.js starts it on
           intersection and pauses it off-screen, and leaves it paused
           entirely under prefers-reduced-motion. -->
      <figure class="why__film">
        <video id="whyFilm" muted loop playsinline preload="none"
               poster="/vid/film-poster.jpg">
          <source src="/vid/film.webm" type="video/webm">
          <source src="/vid/film.mp4"  type="video/mp4">
        </video>
        <figcaption class="why__cap">On the set</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>
  </div>
</section>
